# Reusable Queries: Segments, Projections, Orderings, and Views

How can a UI, export, and scheduled job share a query without copying SQL?
Put reusable intent in the domain's query library. This database-free workbook
uses the shipped DSL, compiles real SQL, and checks invalid input handling.
It does not implement storage, a scheduler, or an authorization service.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_core!()
```

## 1) Name the business concepts

A segment filters rows; a projection controls output; an ordering makes the
sequence deterministic; a view combines them. These are query definitions,
not database `CREATE VIEW` objects or precomputed results.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions

defmodule OrderLibrary do
  use Selecto.QueryLibrary.DSL

  defsegment open_orders do
    where(:status, eq: "open")
  end

  defsegment minimum_priority do
    param(:minimum, :integer)
    where(:priority, gte: param(:minimum))
  end

  defsegment actionable do
    all_of do
      segment(:open_orders)
      segment(:minimum_priority)
    end
  end

  defprojection identity do
    fields([:id, :reference])
  end

  defprojection summary do
    include_projection(:identity)
    fields([:status, :priority])
  end

  defordering highest_priority do
    order_by(:priority, :desc)
    order_by(:id, :asc)
  end

  defview work_queue do
    segment(:actionable)
    projection(:summary)
    ordering(:highest_priority)
  end
end

domain = %{
  name: "Reusable order queries",
  source: %{
    source_table: "orders",
    primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :reference, :status, :priority, :visible],
    redact_fields: [],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer}, reference: %{type: :string},
      status: %{type: :string}, priority: %{type: :integer}, visible: %{type: :boolean}
    },
    associations: %{}
  },
  schemas: %{}, joins: %{},
  required_filters: [{"visible", true}],
  required_selected: [:id],
  query_library: OrderLibrary.query_library()
}

runtime = Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only)
base = Selecto.configure(domain, runtime, mode: :strict)
```

## 2) Apply a view with typed external parameters

The integer parameter is cast from form input. The required visibility filter
stays outside the optional segment; applying a view cannot remove it.

```elixir
queue = Selecto.apply_view(base, :work_queue, minimum: "2")
{sql, params} = Selecto.to_sql(queue)
assert true in params
assert "open" in params
assert 2 in params
assert {"visible", true} in Selecto.query_filters(queue)
assert queue.set.order_by == [{:priority, :desc}, {:id, :asc}]
assert queue.set.selected == [:id, :reference, :status, :priority]
assert Selecto.applied_query_library(queue).views == ["work_queue"]
%{sql: sql, params: params, applied: Selecto.applied_query_library(queue)}
```

The applied-definition names are useful provenance. They are not security
tokens, and this Elixir example does not claim they are emitted as SQL comments.

## 3) Reuse just the parts you need

An export can select identity fields without the full UI view. A projection
replaces optional selections but preserves required domain selections. An
ordering replaces optional ordering; `select/2` and `order_by/2` normally
compose instead. Rebuild from `base` when choosing a different view.

```elixir
export_query =
  base
  |> Selecto.apply_segment(:open_orders)
  |> Selecto.apply_projection(:identity)
  |> Selecto.apply_ordering(:highest_priority)

assert export_query.set.selected == [:id, :reference]
assert {"visible", true} in Selecto.query_filters(export_query)
assert base.set.filtered == []
{export_sql, export_params} = Selecto.to_sql(export_query)
%{sql: export_sql, params: export_params}
```

## 4) Reject invalid or incomplete requests before execution

```elixir
assert_raise ArgumentError, fn -> Selecto.apply_view(base, :missing_view) end
assert_raise ArgumentError, fn -> Selecto.apply_view(base, :work_queue) end
assert_raise ArgumentError, fn ->
  Selecto.apply_view(base, :work_queue, minimum: "not-an-integer")
end
assert_raise ArgumentError, fn ->
  Selecto.apply_view(base, :work_queue, minimum: 2, unexpected: "value")
end
```

## 5) Store intent, not a connection or executable SQL

An application can store a versioned request like this and reapply it against
its currently authorized domain. This is a minimal request format, **not** a
built-in persistence API. Validate versions, authorization, and parameter
limits on every load; never deserialize a trusted runtime or executable code.

```elixir
saved_request = %{
  "version" => 1,
  "view" => "work_queue",
  "params" => %{"minimum" => "3"}
}

assert saved_request["version"] == 1
assert saved_request["view"] in ["work_queue"]
restored = Selecto.apply_view(base, saved_request["view"], saved_request["params"])
{_restored_sql, restored_params} = Selecto.to_sql(restored)
assert 3 in restored_params
assert true in restored_params
```

Extend this with tenant context supplied by the host, not saved form input:
[tenant-scoped reads](selecto_tenant_reads_workbook.livemd). For nested reusable
projections, combine the query library with
[selection shapes and subselects](selecto_selection_shapes_subselects_retargets.livemd).
